<section class="method-section reveal" id="how-it-works">
  <div class="method-intro">
    <div class="eyebrow">Fractional Offset Mechanics</div>
    <h2>Understanding IST Offset and Longitude Lines</h2>
    <p>
      To convert Greenwich Mean Time to Indian Standard Time, you must add 5 hours and 30 minutes to the standard reference clock. The formula is written as follows:
    </p>
    <p>
      <code>IST = GMT + 5 Hours 30 Minutes</code>
    </p>
    <p>
      India selected the meridian of 82.5° East longitude (passing through Mirzapur near Allahabad) as its standard time baseline. This meridian is exactly 5.5 hours ahead of the prime meridian in Greenwich.
    </p>
    <p>
      Because India does not observe daylight saving time, the offset remains exactly +5:30 year-round. This half-hour fractional offset requires careful integration in database engines and calendar booking APIs.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="method-grid">
    <div class="method-card">
      <h3>Greenwich Mean Time</h3>
      <code>UTC+0 Baseline</code>
      <p>GMT is the solar time standard centered on the Royal Observatory in Greenwich, London. It does not observe daylight saving shifts.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="method-card">
      <h3>Indian Standard Time</h3>
      <code>UTC+5:30 Offset</code>
      <p>IST is active year-round. It operates exactly 5.5 hours ahead of GMT, providing constant timezone synchronization.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="method-card">
      <h3>82.5° E Longitude</h3>
      <code>Mirzapur Meridian</code>
      <p>The fractional half-hour offset exists because India standardizes its time based on the central longitude meridian.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

