<section id="topics" class="reveal">
  <h2>History and Longitude Meridian of Indian Standard Time</h2>
  <div class="content">
    <article>
      <h3>Why India Standard Time uses UTC+5:30</h3>
      <p>
        The origin of standard time in India traces back to Madras Time, established by the East India Company in 1805. Under British rule, regional time meridians were observed in Mumbai (Bombay Time) and Kolkata (Calcutta Time), which had a 33-minute difference.
      </p>
      <p>
        In 1906, standard time was standardized on the 82.5° East longitude meridian passing through the observatory in Mirzapur. Selecting a central meridian situated halfway between standard UTC offsets produced the unique UTC+5:30 fractional offset.
      </p>
    </article>
    <article>
      <h3>Why India maintains a Single Time Zone</h3>
      <p>
        India's geographical width spans nearly 30 degrees of longitude, meaning the sun rises almost two hours earlier in the northeastern state of Assam than in western Gujarat. While regional groups have campaigned for a second time zone (often called "Chaibagaan time") to save daylight in the east, the Union government maintains a single time zone.
      </p>
      <p>
        A unified clock prevents rail and flight collisions, simplifies banking systems, and coordinates federal administration.
      </p>
    </article>
  </div>

  <h2>GMT to IST Remote Work &amp; SaaS Integrations</h2>
  <div class="content">
    <article>
      <h3>Collaborating with Indian Software Teams</h3>
      <p>
        Bengaluru and Hyderabad house some of the largest software engineering centers in the world. Developers routinely push code, deploy API microservices, and manage database pipelines while coordinating tasks with project managers in London.
      </p>
      <p>
        The standard workday overlap occurs between 8:30 AM GMT and 12:30 PM GMT (which is 2:00 PM IST and 6:00 PM IST). Teams leverage these 4 hours for live troubleshooting, code reviews, and project syncs.
      </p>
    </article>
    <article>
      <h3>Cloud Deployment and Support Windows</h3>
      <p>
        Because India does not observe daylight saving, scheduled system maintenance windows are predictable year-round. Network operations centers (NOCs) typically target 9:30 PM GMT (3:00 AM IST the next day) for large cloud infrastructure migrations to minimize disruptions.
      </p>
    </article>
  </div>
</section>

