<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     COMMON CONFUSIONS
     ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<section id="source-notes" class="source-notes reveal">
  <h2>Common Confusions: IST, GMT, UTC, and Israel/Irish Time</h2>
  <p>
    Timezone abbreviations are easily confused. Let's clarify the key definitions and differences:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>IST vs. Israel Standard Time</strong>: Both share the "IST" acronym. However, Israel Standard Time is UTC+2 (standard) or UTC+3 (summer). Indian Standard Time is UTC+5:30 year-round. It is crucial to verify which zone is intended during Middle Eastern business scheduling.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>IST vs. Irish Standard Time</strong>: Irish Standard Time is observed in Ireland during summer daylight saving hours (UTC+1). Indian Standard Time is always UTC+5:30.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>UTC+5 vs. UTC+5:30</strong>: Standard standard time zones are aligned to whole-hour meridians. Fractional zones like India (UTC+5:30) and Nepal (UTC+5:45) use fractional meridians to match local solar cycles more closely.
    </li>
  </ul>

